Boss: "Hey SB, Users are reporting strange account lockouts. So as my SOC tier 1 that I appriecate so much and any company would be so happy to have because you work really hard and your trying your best, please fetch me those failed logins."

SB: "OK Boss that employs me and gives me money to buy food and cat toys, I am on it! WAIT whats the ip address?"

Boss: "it's 10.10.10.25" 

Goal:
Download the authentication log and look for failed logins.

Step 1: Open a Terminal

I'm already logged on as sara@workstation:~$ so I know I'm in my home directory.

Step 2: Connect to the FTP Server

My boss told me the logs are stored on: 10.10.10.25

So I type:

ftp 10.10.10.25 into the terminal and press Enter.

The server replies:

Connected to 10.10.10.25
220 FTP Server Ready
Name:

Step 3: Log In

I know the username is: analyst

So I type: analyst and Press Enter.

The server asks: Password:

I dont know the password so I just Press Enter, because there's a good chance my account has access to this server already. It works! 

The sever says:

230 Login successful
ftp>

I notice the prompt changed from:

sara@workstation:~$

to

ftp>

this means I'm now talking to the FTP server.

Step 4: Look Around

I need to know what files are available.

I type:

ls

Server responds:

auth.log
web.log
firewall.log
backup.zip

Now I know there's an authentication log.

Step 5: Download the Log

I want:

auth.log

So I type:

get auth.log

Server responds:

local: auth.log remote: auth.log
226 Transfer complete

Translation:

"I copied auth.log from the server to your computer."

Step 6: Leave FTP

I'm done transferring files.

I Type:

quit

Server responds:

221 Goodbye

My prompt changes back:

sara@workstation:~$

Now I'm no longer connected to FTP.

Step 7: Verify the File Exists

I type:

ls

Output:

auth.log

Now I can physically see the file sitting in my current directory.

Step 8: Read the Log

I type:

cat auth.log

I see:

Nov 13 Failed password for root
Nov 13 Failed password for admin
Nov 13 Failed password for guest
Nov 13 Accepted password for sara
Step 9: Find Only Failed Logins

The file is huge.

Instead of reading thousands of lines, I type:

grep "Failed" auth.log

Output:

Nov 13 Failed password for root
Nov 13 Failed password for admin
Nov 13 Failed password for guest

Now I've filtered the log.

Step 10: Report Back

My findings:

root had failed login attempts
admin had failed login attempts
guest had failed login attempts

This might indicate:

a password spraying attack
credential stuffing
someone guessing passwords

